                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "2b210fa1-dd36-3905-857a-be51ef119e35",
                        "entry_id": "cdc117bf-a24c-31fb-9e6b-30bc60c91c6b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Nom vernaculaire de l’Astrocaryum vulgare, palmier au tronc épineux de la famille des Arécacées (Arecaceae), originaire de la côte nord du Brésil et de la Guyane. Son fruit est comestible; on mange aussi les graines épluchées en \\\"bouillon d'aouara\\\" (sorte de ragoût). (Guyane)"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "71aacd6a-d95d-31ce-a535-dac83256bcf0",
                        "entry_id": "cdc117bf-a24c-31fb-9e6b-30bc60c91c6b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Fruit de cet arbre."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5ffe18c7-c9ff-48a8-aebe-343b784b5018",
                                "sense_id": "71aacd6a-d95d-31ce-a535-dac83256bcf0",
                                "text": "Les pâturages nourrissent le bétail et l’aouara nourrit les porcs."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "0b926f0b-3240-4a6f-a757-ce48b6d7a0b3",
                        "source_entry_id": "cdc117bf-a24c-31fb-9e6b-30bc60c91c6b",
                        "target_lang_word": "awarra",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "awarra",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "56cca2e7-8fa5-3f76-824c-65a4b75421ec",
                                        "entry_id": "657fcc42-6de8-3c8a-bc52-f87bc8647d8c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternative spelling of awara."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "alternative"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "40876c78-a7ea-43a5-b863-512a19e40f66",
                                                "sense_id": "56cca2e7-8fa5-3f76-824c-65a4b75421ec",
                                                "text": "The food found in its stomach was the seed of the awarra, a species of palm (Aristocaryon spec?)"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e7223290-7843-43fb-82a9-a2fe03625a00",
                                                "sense_id": "56cca2e7-8fa5-3f76-824c-65a4b75421ec",
                                                "text": "With this prepared cutting-pole they succeeded, after a while, in detaching the cluster of palms, which fell heavily to the ground, scattering the awarra nuts in all directions. But with the bunch of awarras came also mother coomby with her brood of seven young ones on her back,—their long prehensile tails firmly entwined around the tail of the old lady."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "46b36a91-fac8-4411-8441-e755650c476a",
                                                "sense_id": "56cca2e7-8fa5-3f76-824c-65a4b75421ec",
                                                "text": "The five principal palms of the British Guiana rivers are the trooly, the aeta, the cocorite, the manicole, and the awarra, and all these grow in the greatest profusion on the banks of the Berbice."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e23f2896-5ea4-48d8-82d5-735f67fdffa2",
                                                "sense_id": "56cca2e7-8fa5-3f76-824c-65a4b75421ec",
                                                "text": "Above Ahwiemah, and upwards until the sandy ridges in the neighbourhood of Youacourie creek are met with, the district is almost a continuous swamp, in which the prickly awarra palms (Astrocaryum) luxuriate and in some places completely line the riverside."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "238b60d7-eff7-4645-9cf4-b66dddf0a2f6",
                                                "sense_id": "56cca2e7-8fa5-3f76-824c-65a4b75421ec",
                                                "text": "An Amerindian informant living in the area stated that it fed on fruits of the Awarra Palm Astrocaryum tucumoides and the Cuyuru Palm A. tucuma."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d312ba9f-ba00-4491-9a32-d9ae3f55b2f0",
                                                "sense_id": "56cca2e7-8fa5-3f76-824c-65a4b75421ec",
                                                "text": "As a punishment, they put him to sit on top of a tall awarra palm. The trunk of the tree was thickly covered with sharp thorns, which prevented him from climbing down. [...] One day, as a group of spiders arrived to eat the ripe yellow awarra on the tree, they were shocked to find Kamoa sitting among the branches."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
